{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017sADrsff7aDQmf4qYEPk49","type":"tool_result","content":"// Package wtlock serialises git worktree administration (worktree add,\n// remove, prune) per repository within this process. git's worktree\n// bookkeeping under <common-dir>/worktrees is not safe against concurrent\n// admin commands (#109). Callers hold the lock only for the admin command\n// itself; fetch, commit and push never take it.\npackage wtlock\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sync\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\tmu    sync.Mutex\n\tlocks = map[string]chan struct{}{}\n)\n\nfunc slot(key string) chan struct{} {\n\tmu.Lock()\n\tdefer mu.Unlock()\n\tc, ok := locks[key]\n\tif !ok {\n\t\tc = make(chan struct{}, 1)\n\t\tlocks[key] = c\n\t}\n\treturn c\n}\n\n// Lock blocks until this process holds the worktree-administration lock for\n// the repository whose common git directory is commonDir, or ctx is done.\n// commonDir is made absolute and its symlinks resolved, so two spellings of\n// one directory share a lock. unlock is idempotent and releases only this\n// holder's acquisition.\nfunc Lock(ctx context.Context, commonDir string) (unlock func(), err error) {\n\tabs, err := filepath.Abs(commonDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: %s: %w\", commonDir, err)\n\t}\n\tkey, err := filepath.EvalSymlinks(abs)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: %s: %w\", commonDir, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: wait for %s: %w\", key, err)\n\t}\n\tc := slot(key)\n\tselect {\n\tcase c <- struct{}{}:\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: wait for %s: %w\", key, ctx.Err())\n\t}\n\tvar once sync.Once\n\treturn func() { once.Do(func() { <-c }) }, nil\n}\ninternal/gitx/gitx_test.go:329:\tunlock, err := g.worktreeLock(ctx, clone)\ninternal/gitx/gitx_test.go:336:\tif _, err := g.worktreeLock(short, linked); !errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded) {\ninternal/gitx/gitx_test.go:339:\tu2, err := g.worktreeLock(ctx, other)\ninternal/gitx/local.go:20:// worktreeAdminTimeout bounds each worktree add, remove and prune that runs\ninternal/gitx/local.go:22:const worktreeAdminTimeout = 30 * time.Second\ninternal/gitx/local.go:24:// worktreeLock takes the process-wide worktree-administration lock for the\ninternal/gitx/local.go:26:func (g *Git) worktreeLock(ctx context.Context, repoDir string) (func(), error) {\ninternal/gitx/local.go:83:\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, worktreeAdminTimeout)\ninternal/gitx/local.go:85:\tunlock, err := g.worktreeLock(ctx, repoDir)\ninternal/gitx/local.go:102:\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.WithoutCancel(ctx), worktreeAdminTimeout)\ninternal/gitx/local.go:104:\tunlock, err := g.worktreeLock(ctx, repoDir)\ninternal/gitx/local.go:133:func (g *Git) fetch(ctx context.Context, repoDir, url, refspec string, token TokenFunc, shallow bool) error {\n306:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e208eb5f-d25d-4e32-abd6-d040c1d77682","uuid":"fcb09f9a-7ec1-4066-8ddc-58fc7e34fef3","timestamp":"2026-11-25T14:04:58.652Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"// Package wtlock serialises git worktree administration (worktree add,\n// remove, prune) per repository within this process. git's worktree\n// bookkeeping under <common-dir>/worktrees is not safe against concurrent\n// admin commands (#109). Callers hold the lock only for the admin command\n// itself; fetch, commit and push never take it.\npackage wtlock\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sync\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\tmu    sync.Mutex\n\tlocks = map[string]chan struct{}{}\n)\n\nfunc slot(key string) chan struct{} {\n\tmu.Lock()\n\tdefer mu.Unlock()\n\tc, ok := locks[key]\n\tif !ok {\n\t\tc = make(chan struct{}, 1)\n\t\tlocks[key] = c\n\t}\n\treturn c\n}\n\n// Lock blocks until this process holds the worktree-administration lock for\n// the repository whose common git directory is commonDir, or ctx is done.\n// commonDir is made absolute and its symlinks resolved, so two spellings of\n// one directory share a lock. unlock is idempotent and releases only this\n// holder's acquisition.\nfunc Lock(ctx context.Context, commonDir string) (unlock func(), err error) {\n\tabs, err := filepath.Abs(commonDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: %s: %w\", commonDir, err)\n\t}\n\tkey, err := filepath.EvalSymlinks(abs)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: %s: %w\", commonDir, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: wait for %s: %w\", key, err)\n\t}\n\tc := slot(key)\n\tselect {\n\tcase c <- struct{}{}:\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"wtlock: wait for %s: %w\", key, ctx.Err())\n\t}\n\tvar once sync.Once\n\treturn func() { once.Do(func() { <-c }) }, nil\n}\ninternal/gitx/gitx_test.go:329:\tunlock