age, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017sADrsff7aDQmf4qYEPk49","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
